    public function getNodeType(): string {
        return CollectedDataNode::class;
    }

    public function processNode(Node $node, Scope $scope): array {
        // $node->getData() contains aggregated collector results
        $data = $node->getData();
        $unusedMethods = $data['unused_methods'] ?? [];

        // Check which methods are actually used...
        // Report unused ones

        return [];
    }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Scope Name Resolution

Source: https://github.com/phpstan/phpstan-src/blob/2.2.x/_autodocs/09-api-quick-reference.md

Resolve names to their fully qualified names or types within the current scope. Use resolveName for AST nodes and resolveTypeByName for type representations.

```php
// Resolve a Name AST node
$fullName = $scope->resolveName($nameNode);  // Returns string

// Get type representation
$type = $scope->resolveTypeByName($nameNode);  // Returns Type
// self → ThisType, static → StaticType, others → ObjectType
```

### API Quick Reference > Scope Methods > Context Queries

Source: https://github.com/phpstan/phpstan-src/blob/2.2.x/_autodocs/09-api-quick-reference.md

Scope methods for context queries allow checking if the current code is within a trait or an anonymous function, and retrieving the corresponding reflection objects. They also provide access to the current class and function context, as well as the PHP version being used.

--------------------------------

### Collectors

Source: https://github.com/phpstan/phpstan-src/blob/2.2.x/_autodocs/03-rules.md

Collectors are used for rules that require cross-file information. They gather data in parallel from AST nodes, and the aggregated results are then processed by a `CollectedDataNode` rule. The `Collector` interface, found in the `PHPStan\`Collectors\` namespace, defines two key methods: `getNodeType()` to specify the AST node type to listen for, and `processNode()` to process each matching node and return collected data.

